    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Cal Raleigh** stands out as our primary engine with a 78.01 efficiency score. Facing Kay, who carries a shaky 5.28 xERA, **Cal Raleigh** benefits from a massive SP skill advantage and a prime #2 spot in the order, making him our most reliable asset for power production. Right behind him, **Lawrence Butler** (73.50) is the highest-upside play in the outfield. Despite hitting seventh, his 16% park factor boost and 13% platoon edge against Baz turn him into a potential difference-maker. We are also leaning heavily into the matchup against Civale, which elevates both **Taylor Ward** (68.38) and **Jeremiah Jackson** (62.54); while the latter\u2019s #9 spot hurts his volume, his park-adjusted potential makes him a viable start in a high-scoring environment.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Bryson Stott**. He narrowly missed the cut with a 46.55 score, ultimately sidelined by a unfavorable platoon split against the left-handed Freeland and a suboptimal #9 batting position that effectively capped his ceiling. Meanwhile, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains in the lineup despite a sub-60 score (56.56) due to the sheer lack of high-floor alternatives, though he faces a stiff challenge against Ryan\u2019s 3.40 xERA. We are also keeping a watchful eye on **Daulton Varsho**, whose score of 69.32 is bolstered by the matchup against Kochanowicz, though his efficiency is slightly tempered by the opposing catcher's elite ability to control the run game, creating a slight drag on his SB upside. No weather-related risks are currently forecasted, but we are proceeding without **Yainer Diaz**, who remains sidelined on the IL.",
